{
  "fmus": {
    "node": "DemoCanNodeTriggeredOutput.fmu",
    "bus": "DemoCanBusSimulation.fmu"
  },
  "topology": "node1.CanChannel = bus.Node1, node2.CanChannel = bus.Node2; two instances of the node FMU attached to the two terminals of one bus simulation FMU",
  "profile": "application/org.fmi-standard.fmi-ls-bus.can",
  "why": "Stated from the two upstream FMUs' sources and the FMI-LS-BUS headers, not from a Run. The node's App_Instantiate creates one CAN baudrate and one arbitration-lost-behavior configuration operation and raises the Tx clock; App_DoStep creates one CAN transmit operation, ID 0x1 with payload 01 02 03 04, for every 300 ms boundary the step crosses. The bus's App_EvaluateDiscreteStates queues each frame it is handed, enables communication once both terminals configured one baud rate, and schedules the next transmission by stating a countdown interval on both Tx clocks. When they are activated it transmits the queued frame of lowest CAN ID, answering its originator with a Confirm operation and handing the frame itself to the other terminal, then schedules the next queued frame. Both nodes offer ID 0x1, so node1's frame wins the first arbitration and node2's the second.",
  "transmission": {
    "frame_bits": 44,
    "data_length": 4,
    "baudrate": 100000,
    "interval_ns": 480000,
    "why": "The bus states its Tx clocks' interval as the exact fraction (44 + dataLength) / baudRate seconds, where 44 is the constant length of a CAN frame in bits: 48/100000 s, which is 480 000 whole nanoseconds. No step period a Manifest is likely to declare lands on that instant, so the group is what has to stop there."
  },
  "no_next_event_time": "Neither FMU declares a next event time: the node communicates through a triggered output Clock and the bus through countdown input Clocks, so every instant either of them asks for is asked for by a Clock.",
